.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1200 AM PDT Sat Aug 1 2026
(Today and tonight)

What a difference 24 hrs makes. Last night we were monitoring heat
signatures from the Woodside Incident and a few patches of stratus
on the satellite. Tonight no heat signatures and stratus came back
with solid coverage over the coastal waters and locally inland.
Near-term hi-res models have a decent handle on the current
stratus pattern. The marine layer has deepened overnight to near
800-1000 feet. Expect stratus to linger into the morning then fade
pretty quick as a weak cold front approaches from the north. This
cold front will be dry in nature, but help mix out the marine
layer and set the stage for a rapid increase in N-S gradient. The
bigger impact to today weather wise will be the heat, especially
the heat across the far interior. Regions that are being impacted
by the marine layer have the best chance of fully tapping into the
850mb temps in the upper 20C range. That would push temps above
100 again with the hotspots going for 110. Widespread HeatRisk of
Moderate with some Major across interior Central Coast. Simply
put, if you`re doing outdoor activities away from the coast today
be mindful and take some heat safety precautions. If you`re
seeking relief from the heat and want the natural AC at the coast
you`ll get it with highs in the 60s to 80s. Still energetic surf
zone so use caution at the beaches. Beaches section below for more
info.

By this evening and early tonight the cold front will enter the
northern waters, but washout. The fading front will help to
establish a rapidly increasing N-S gradient from SFO-ACV.
Previous projections put the gradient near -9mb, but tonight`s
model guidance have eased just under -8mb. Despite the drop, the
increasing gradient will mix out the stratus and ramp up the wind.
Hi-res guidance still shows some dangerous Storm Force gusts
north of Pt Reyes. Thankfully the strong winds stay mostly
parallel to the coast. Aiding in less clouds tonight will also
come from a weak burst of offshore flow over the N Bay and Central
Coast.

